  "id": "Compound::DB00458 - Gene::1133",
  "verbalization": "Imipramine, a tricyclic antidepressant, is shown to bind to Chrm2, a receptor that participates in multiple receptor activity categories, including G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity, neurotransmitter receptor activity, and acetylcholine receptor activity. These activities are all linked to Chrm5, suggesting a potential mechanism by which Imipramine influences Chrm5 function. The evidence indicates that Imipramine may modulate Chrm5 through a pathway involving Chrm2, which is a key component of the acetylcholine receptor system. The overlapping participation of Chrm2 in different receptor activity categories implies that Imipramine could exert its effects on Chrm5 by targeting a common intermediary, Chrm2, thereby influencing the downstream signaling of the acetylcholine receptor. The consistent binding of Imipramine to Chrm2 across the three paths supports a direct interaction, and the shared participation of Chrm2 in receptor activity suggests a broader functional link between Imipramine and Chrm5.",
